## 3. Walkthrough & Exam Drills

### Drill 1: Services & CoreDNS Discovery (`01-services/`)

A Kubernetes Service provides a stable network abstraction (virtual ClusterIP and DNS name) fronting dynamic, ephemeral
pods. In this drill, master manual imperative creation, multi-port definitions, EndpointSlice tracking, and Headless
services.

#### Step 1: Deploy Backend Workload

Navigate to the services directory and inspect the workload deployment:

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_networking_dir }}/01-services
cat cka-lab-backend-app.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-backend-app.yaml
kubectl rollout status deployment/cka-lab-backend-app -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }} --timeout=60s
```

The backend pods expose two container ports: port `80` (`http-web`) and port `8080` (`http-metrics`).

#### Step 2: Create a ClusterIP Service (Imperative vs Declarative)

During the CKA exam, use `kubectl expose` to generate Service manifests in seconds rather than handwriting YAML
boilerplate from scratch:

```bash
# Imperative dry-run generation
kubectl expose deployment cka-lab-backend-app \
  --name=cka-lab-clusterip-svc \
  --port=80 \
  --target-port=80 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> If a Service exposes multiple ports, Kubernetes **requires** each port entry to have a unique `name` attribute (e.g.,
> `http-web` and `http-metrics`).

Apply the multi-port Service manifest:

```bash
cat cka-lab-clusterip-service.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-clusterip-service.yaml -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
```

Verify service registration and inspect its automatically generated EndpointSlices:

```bash
kubectl get svc cka-lab-clusterip-svc -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
kubectl get endpointslices -l "kubernetes.io/service-name=cka-lab-clusterip-svc" -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
```

#### Step 3: Test CoreDNS Resolution & ClusterIP Connectivity

In the exam, test reachability and DNS lookups by launching a quick ephemeral troubleshooting pod:

```bash
# 1. Verify CoreDNS discovery (resolves <service>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local)
kubectl run cka-lab-dns-probe \
  --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
  --rm -i --restart=Never \
  -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -- nslookup cka-lab-clusterip-svc.{{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}.svc.cluster.local

# 2. Verify HTTP traffic flow to ClusterIP
CLUSTER_IP=$(kubectl get svc cka-lab-clusterip-svc -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }} -o jsonpath='{.spec.clusterIP}')
kubectl run cka-lab-http-probe \
  --image=busybox:1.36.1 \
  --rm -i --restart=Never \
  -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }} \
  -- wget -qO- "http://${CLUSTER_IP}:80"
```

#### Step 4: Expose Service Externally via NodePort

A `NodePort` service allocates a static port from the cluster range (default `30000-32767`) on **every node**. Incoming
traffic to `<NodeIP>:<NodePort>` is proxied directly to matching pods:

```bash
# Imperative dry-run creation
kubectl expose deployment cka-lab-backend-app \
  --name=cka-lab-nodeport-svc \
  --type=NodePort \
  --port=80 \
  --target-port=80 \
  --dry-run=client -o yaml
```

Apply the multi-port NodePort manifest with pinned ports `31080` (web) and `31090` (metrics):

```bash
cat cka-lab-nodeport-service.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-nodeport-service.yaml -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
kubectl get svc cka-lab-nodeport-svc -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
```

Test external reachability by probing any cluster worker node IP on port `31080`:

```bash
NODE_IP=$(kubectl get nodes -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.addresses[?(@.type=="InternalIP")].address}')
curl -s "http://${NODE_IP}:31080" | grep -i "nginx"
```

#### Step 5: Headless Service (`clusterIP: None`) for Direct Pod Addressing

When `spec.clusterIP: None` is configured, Kubernetes skips allocating a virtual ClusterIP. Instead, CoreDNS returns
direct `A` records for each matching Pod IP:

```bash
cat cka-lab-headless-service.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-headless-service.yaml
kubectl rollout status statefulset/cka-headless-app --timeout=60s
```

Verify that CoreDNS resolves the service name to the individual Pod IPs instead of a single virtual IP:

```bash
# Resolves all backing StatefulSet Pod IPs directly
kubectl run test-headless-dns --rm -i --restart=Never --image=busybox:1.36 -- \
  nslookup cka-headless-svc.{{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}.svc.cluster.local

# Resolves specific StatefulSet pod identity (<pod-name>.<service-name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local)
kubectl run test-pod-dns --rm -i --restart=Never --image=busybox:1.36 -- \
  nslookup cka-headless-app-0.cka-headless-svc.{{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}.svc.cluster.local
```

#### Step 6: Node-Local Routing (`PreferSameNode` - Kubernetes 1.35+)

In Kubernetes 1.35, the `spec.trafficDistribution` field allows Services to favor routing to endpoints residing on the
same node as the client pod, eliminating unnecessary cross-node latency:

```bash
cat cka-lab-traffic-distribution-svc.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-traffic-distribution-svc.yaml -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }}
kubectl describe service cka-lab-traffic-distribution-svc -n {{ cka_lab_networking_namespace | default('default') }} | grep -i "Traffic Distribution"
```

#### Step 7: Run Automated Verification for Module 1

```bash
./test-services.sh
./test-headless.sh
```

