### Drill 4: Network Policies with httpbin-go (`04-network-policies/`)

> [!NOTE]
> **No Extra Helm Installation Required**: NetworkPolicies are enforced natively by Cilium CNI (already active in the
> cluster). Workload pods (`cka-lab-target-backend` and clients) are created directly by the manifests in this drill.

NetworkPolicies enforce pod-to-pod microsegmentation. Understand the core rules:

1. **Non-Isolated by Default**: If no policy selects a pod, it accepts traffic from all sources.
2. **Isolation on Selection**: As soon as `spec.podSelector` matches a pod, that pod drops all unwhitelisted traffic for
   the declared `policyTypes` (`Ingress` / `Egress`).
3. **Whitelist Model**: NetworkPolicies only grant access; they cannot explicitly deny.

#### Step 1: Provision Namespaces and Workloads

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_networking_dir }}/04-network-policies
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-namespaces.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-backend-workload.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-client-workloads.yaml

kubectl rollout status deployment/cka-lab-target-backend -n {{ cka_lab_networking_backend_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-backend') }} --timeout=60s
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/cka-lab-client-authorized -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} --timeout=60s
kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/cka-lab-client-unauthorized -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} --timeout=60s
```

#### Step 2: Test Baseline Connectivity (Expect Success)

Prior to applying any policy, both client pods can reach the backend service:

```bash
BACKEND_URL="http://cka-lab-target-backend-svc.{{ cka_lab_networking_backend_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-backend') }}.svc.cluster.local:80"

kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} cka-lab-client-authorized -- wget -qO- -T 3 "${BACKEND_URL}"
kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} cka-lab-client-unauthorized -- wget -qO- -T 3 "${BACKEND_URL}"
```

#### Step 3: Apply Default Deny Ingress Policy

```bash
cat cka-lab-default-deny-ingress.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-default-deny-ingress.yaml
```

The empty `podSelector: {}` isolates **all pods** in namespace `{{ cka_lab_networking_backend_namespace |
default('cka-lab-net-backend') }}`. Verify that connections now time out:

```bash
kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} cka-lab-client-authorized -- wget -qO- -T 2 "${BACKEND_URL}" || echo "Connection dropped by default-deny!"
```

#### Step 4: Whitelist Authorized Clients via `namespaceSelector` & `podSelector`

```bash
cat cka-lab-allow-client-to-backend.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-allow-client-to-backend.yaml
```

Inspect the `from:` block:

```yaml
ingress:
  - from:
      - namespaceSelector:
          matchLabels:
            kubernetes.io/metadata.name: {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }}
        podSelector:
          matchLabels:
            access: authorized
```

Placing `namespaceSelector` and `podSelector` together in the **same list item** creates a logical **AND** condition.

#### Step 5: Whitelist CoreDNS Egress (Avoid CKA Exam Trap)

When restricting egress traffic, you **must** allow traffic to CoreDNS (UDP/TCP port 53), otherwise all DNS lookups will
fail:

```bash
cat cka-lab-egress-dns-policy.yaml
kubectl apply -f cka-lab-egress-dns-policy.yaml
```

#### Step 6: Verify Selective Enforcement

```bash
# 1. Authorized client succeeds
kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} cka-lab-client-authorized -- wget -qO- -T 3 "${BACKEND_URL}"

# 2. Unauthorized client is blocked
kubectl exec -n {{ cka_lab_networking_client_namespace | default('cka-lab-net-client') }} cka-lab-client-unauthorized -- wget -qO- -T 2 "${BACKEND_URL}" || echo "Unauthorized client blocked!"
```

#### Step 7: Run Automated Verification for Module 4

```bash
./test-network-policies.sh
```

